--[[
  tts.lua - Text-to-speech for CC: Tweaked speakers (engine + console)
  -------------------------------------------------------------------
  A formant speech synthesizer in pure Lua with a built-in control console.
  It produces real (robotic) spoken English through speaker.playAudio,
  not note-block beeps.

  As a program:
    tts                       open the Speech Synthesis Console (Advanced
                              Computer); plain text prompt on other computers
    tts -t                    plain text prompt even on an Advanced Computer
    tts Hello world           speak the arguments
    tts --natural Hello       natural voice (default when tts_voice.dat is present)
    tts --male Hello          robotic male voice (--female: robotic female)
    tts -p 90 -s 0.9 Hi       pitch 90 Hz, speed 0.9
    tts -v 2 Loud message     volume 0..3
    tts -f story.txt          read a text file aloud

  As a library:
    local tts = require("tts")
    tts.say("The reactor is overheating!")
    tts.say("Hello", { voice = "female", speed = 1.1, volume = 1.5 })
    tts.settings.voice = "female"            -- change the default voice
    tts.addWord("creeper", "K R IY1 P ER")   -- custom pronunciation
    tts.gui()                                -- open the console yourself

  Requires CC: Tweaked 1.100+ (speaker.playAudio) and one or more speakers
  attached or connected through wired modems.

  Natural voice (best): upload tts_voice_big.dat to a public GitHub
  repository, then run once:   tts url <link to the file>
  The program downloads it into memory at start-up (needs internet access),
  so it takes no disk space. It holds ~12 minutes of recorded speech from
  the openly licensed Kokoro "af_heart" voice; the program picks the
  recorded stretches that best fit each sentence.

  Natural voice (offline backup): tts_voice.dat in the same folder as
  tts.lua. Smaller and a little more robotic. Without either, the program
  uses the robotic voices.
]]

